    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the heads up. the thing is, they don't really act like normal mice. it's more like... i'll be working on a specific joint, and right as i'm about to tighten it, i'll hear this little scratching sound right next to my ear, or a tiny piece of hardware i just laid down seems to have rolled an inch away. it feels less like they're looking for food and more like they're just... being a nuisance? like they're intentionally causing these tiny, annoying little glitches in my work. is there any kind of pest or environmental issue that behaves in that kind of erratic, almost \"playful\" way?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Erratic, almost \"playful\" behavior in pests is less common, but when you're dealing with something that seems more mischievous than hungry, you might be dealing with a very small, highly agitated creature, or perhaps even something more unusual. In the context of a tight, dark space, tiny, fast-moving things that seem to be poking at things could be woodlice, very small insects, or even juvenile rodents that are just exploring. It's much more likely to be a behavioral quirk of a stressed pest than true intentional mischief, but it certainly warrants a thorough inspection. If they are indeed small enough to cause hardware to roll, they are certainly capable of causing minor disruption.",
